</ul>
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ===================== Problem 1 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="ex3-p1">
<title>6 points</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Evaluate each of the following limits, or show that the limit does not
exist.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="ex3-p1-a">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{xy + y^3}{x^2 + y^2}</m>
(3 points)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The limit <alert>does not exist</alert>. Let
<m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{xy + y^3}{x^2 + y^2}</m> and approach the origin along
two different paths.
</p>
<p>
<em>Along the <m>y</m>-axis</em> (<m>x = 0</m>, <m>y \to 0</m>):
<md>
f(0,y) = \frac{0 + y^3}{0 + y^2} = y \;\longrightarrow\; 0.
</md>
</p>
<p>
<em>Along the line <m>y = x</m></em> (<m>x \to 0</m>):
<md>
f(x,x) = \frac{x^2 + x^3}{x^2 + x^2} = \frac{x^2(1+x)}{2x^2}
= \frac{1+x}{2} \;\longrightarrow\; \frac12.
</md>
</p>
<p>
The two paths give two different values (<m>0 \ne \frac12</m>), so by the
two-path test the limit does not exist.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="ex3-p1-b">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} \frac{x^2 - xy}{x^2 - y^2}</m>
(3 points)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Substituting <m>(1,1)</m> gives <m>\frac00</m>, so we simplify first. The
function is defined where <m>x^2 \ne y^2</m>, that is, away from the lines
<m>y = x</m> and <m>y = -x</m>. Factor the numerator and the denominator:
<md>
\frac{x^2 - xy}{x^2 - y^2} = \frac{x\,(x-y)}{(x-y)(x+y)} = \frac{x}{x+y}
</md>
at every point of the domain, since <m>x - y \ne 0</m> there. The function
<m>\dfrac{x}{x+y}</m> is continuous at <m>(1,1)</m>, because its denominator
is <m>2 \ne 0</m> there, so
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} \frac{x^2 - xy}{x^2 - y^2}
= \lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} \frac{x}{x+y} = \frac{1}{1+1} = \boxed{\frac12}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Problem 2 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="ex3-p2">
<title>4 points</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>z = e^{x^2 y}</m>, where <m>x(u,v) = \sqrt{uv}</m> and
<m>y(u,v) = \dfrac{1}{v}</m>. Compute <m>\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial v}</m>.
</p>
<p>
<alert>Your final answer must be fully simplified.</alert>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
By the chain rule,
<md>
\frac{\partial z}{\partial v}
= \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}\,\frac{\partial x}{\partial v}
+ \frac{\partial z}{\partial y}\,\frac{\partial y}{\partial v}.
</md>
The four partial derivatives are
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial z}{\partial x} \amp = 2xy\,e^{x^2 y}, \amp
\frac{\partial z}{\partial y} \amp = x^2 e^{x^2 y},</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial x}{\partial v} \amp = \frac{u}{2\sqrt{uv}}, \amp
\frac{\partial y}{\partial v} \amp = -\frac{1}{v^2}.</mrow>
</md>
Therefore
<md>
\frac{\partial z}{\partial v}
= \left(2xy\,e^{x^2 y}\right)\frac{u}{2\sqrt{uv}}
+ \left(x^2 e^{x^2 y}\right)\left(-\frac{1}{v^2}\right).
</md>
Now write everything in terms of <m>u</m> and <m>v</m>. Since
<m>x^2 y = uv\cdot\frac1v = u</m>, we have <m>e^{x^2 y} = e^u</m>, and
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial z}{\partial v}
\amp = \left(2\sqrt{uv}\cdot\frac1v\cdot e^u\right)\frac{u}{2\sqrt{uv}}
- \frac{uv}{v^2}\,e^u</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{u}{v}\,e^u - \frac{u}{v}\,e^u = \boxed{0}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
<em>Check:</em> <m>x^2 y = u</m>, so <m>z = e^u</m> does not depend on
<m>v</m> at all, which agrees with <m>\partial z/\partial v = 0</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Problem 3 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="ex3-p3">
<title>5 points</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the directional derivative of <m>f(x,y) = x^2 y + y^2</m> at the point
<m>(-1,-1)</m> in the direction of the unit vector
<m>\hat{u} = \left\langle \frac{1}{\sqrt2}, \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<em>The direction.</em> The direction
<m>\hat{u} = \left\langle \frac{1}{\sqrt2}, \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle</m>
is given, and it is already a unit vector:
<m>\|\hat{u}\| = \sqrt{\frac12 + \frac12} = 1</m>. So no normalization is
needed.
</p>
<p>
<em>The gradient.</em> The partial derivatives are
<md>
f_x = 2xy, \qquad f_y = x^2 + 2y.
</md>
At <m>P_0(-1,-1)</m>,
<md>
<mrow>f_x(-1,-1) \amp = 2(-1)(-1) = 2, \qquad f_y(-1,-1) = 1 - 2 = -1,</mrow>
<mrow>\text{so}\quad \left(\nabla f\right)_{P_0} \amp = \langle 2, -1\rangle.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
<em>The directional derivative.</em>
<md>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}
\amp = \left(\nabla f\right)_{P_0}\cdot\hat{u}
= \langle 2,-1\rangle\cdot\left\langle \frac{1}{\sqrt2}, \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{2-1}{\sqrt2}
= \boxed{\frac{1}{\sqrt2} = \frac{\sqrt2}{2}}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Problem 4 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="ex3-p4">
<title>3 points</title>
<statement>
<p>
Identify and sketch the surface corresponding to the following equation:
<md>
z^2 + y^2 - x^2 + 1 = 0.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Rewrite the equation in standard form:
<md>
x^2 - y^2 - z^2 = 1.
</md>
One squared term is positive and two are negative, and the right-hand side
is <m>1</m>. Look at the traces:
<ul>
<li><p><m>z = 0</m>: <m>x^2 - y^2 = 1</m>, a hyperbola in the <m>xy</m>-plane opening along the <m>x</m>-axis;</p></li>
<li><p><m>y = 0</m>: <m>x^2 - z^2 = 1</m>, a hyperbola in the <m>xz</m>-plane opening along the <m>x</m>-axis;</p></li>
<li><p><m>x = c</m>: <m>y^2 + z^2 = c^2 - 1</m>. This is empty when <m>|c| \lt 1</m>, a single point when <m>c = \pm 1</m>, and a circle of radius <m>\sqrt{c^2 - 1}</m> when <m>|c| \gt 1</m>.</p></li>
</ul>
</p>
<p>
There are no points of the surface with <m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m>, so the
surface comes in two separate pieces. It is a <alert>hyperboloid of two
sheets</alert> whose axis is the <m>x</m>-axis, with vertices
<m>(\pm 1, 0, 0)</m>. Its cross-sections perpendicular to the
<m>x</m>-axis are circles.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-ex3-hyperboloid">
<caption>The hyperboloid of two sheets <m>x^2 - y^2 - z^2 = 1</m>: two bowls
opening along the positive and the negative <m>x</m>-axis, with vertices
<m>(\pm 1, 0, 0)</m>.</caption>
<image width="70%">
<shortdescription>A hyperboloid of two sheets along the x axis: two bowls opening away from the origin, with vertices (1,0,0) and (minus 1,0,0).</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
Three-dimensional axes with the x axis pointing toward the viewer and to the
right. A blue bowl opens along the positive x axis from its vertex at
(1,0,0), and a second blue bowl opens along the negative x axis from its
vertex at (minus 1,0,0). Their circular rims face away from the origin, and
there is no surface between the two vertices.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-ex3-hyperboloid"><![CDATA[
// The hyperboloid of two sheets x^2 - y^2 - z^2 = 1, axis along the x-axis,
// vertices (1,0,0) and (-1,0,0); each sheet is parametrized as
// (+-cosh s, sinh s cos t, sinh s sin t), 0 <= s <= 1.35.
import graph3;
// Keep HTML interactive; use vector rendering only for print output.
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(12cm,7cm);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=(2.2,-7,2.6),up=Z);
currentlight=light(gray(0.6),specularfactor=0.2,(2,-5,6),(-2,-4,3));
pen ax=gray(0.25)+linewidth(0.6pt);
real smax=1.25;

triple sheet(pair q, real sgn) {
  return (sgn*cosh(q.x), sinh(q.x)*cos(q.y), sinh(q.x)*sin(q.y));
}
triple right(pair q) { return sheet(q, 1); }
triple left(pair q) { return sheet(q, -1); }
pen face=rgb(0.33,0.5,0.85)+opacity(0.75);
pen mesh=rgb(0.12,0.22,0.5)+linewidth(0.3pt);
draw(surface(right, (0,0), (smax,2pi), 10, 28, Spline), surfacepen=face, meshpen=mesh);
draw(surface(left, (0,0), (smax,2pi), 10, 28, Spline), surfacepen=face, meshpen=mesh);

draw(Label("$x$",1),(-3.0,0,0)--(3.3,0,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$y$",1),(0,-1.4,0)--(0,2.4,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$z$",1),(0,0,-1.9)--(0,0,2.4),ax,Arrow3);

dot((1,0,0), black+linewidth(4pt));
dot((-1,0,0), black+linewidth(4pt));
// the vertex labels sit below the sheets, joined to the vertices by leader lines
pen lead=black+linewidth(0.5pt);
draw((1,0,0)--(1.15,0,-1.7), lead);
draw((-1,0,0)--(-1.15,0,-1.7), lead);
label("$(1,0,0)$", (1.15,0,-1.7), S);
label("$(-1,0,0)$", (-1.15,0,-1.7), S);
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Problem 5 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="ex3-p5">
<title>2 points</title>
<statement>
<p>
Decide whether the statement below is true or false. Clearly justify your
answer.
</p>
<p>
<alert>True or False?</alert> There exists a function <m>f(x,y)</m> such
that
<md>
f_x(x,y) = y \qquad\text{and}\qquad f_y(x,y) = x^2
</md>
at every point <m>(x,y)</m> of the plane.
</p>
<p>
(Assume that <m>f</m> and its partial derivatives <m>f_x</m>,
<m>f_y</m>, <m>f_{xy}</m> and <m>f_{yx}</m> are defined and continuous at
every point of the <m>xy</m>-plane.)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<alert>False.</alert> Suppose such a function <m>f</m> existed. Then
<md>
f_{xy} = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(f_x\right)
= \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(y) = 1
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
f_{yx} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(f_y\right)
= \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(x^2\right) = 2x.
</md>
By assumption <m>f</m>, <m>f_x</m>, <m>f_y</m>, <m>f_{xy}</m> and
<m>f_{yx}</m> are defined and continuous at every point of the plane, so the
Mixed Derivative Theorem (Clairaut's Theorem,
<xref ref="thm-mixed-derivative"/>) applies at every point
<m>(a,b)</m> and gives
<md>
f_{xy}(a,b) = f_{yx}(a,b), \qquad\text{that is,}\qquad 1 = 2a
\quad\text{for every } a.
</md>
This is false (for example, at the origin it says <m>1 = 0</m>). The
contradiction shows that no such function exists.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<p>
<alert>Problems 6 and 7 are optional bonus problems. Together they are
worth 6 points.</alert>
</p>

<!-- ===================== Problem 6 (bonus) ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="ex3-p6">
<title>Bonus, 4 points</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Consider the two planes
<md>
\mathcal P_1:\ x + 2y + 2z = 3
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\mathcal P_2:\ 2x - 2y + z = 3,
</md>
and let <m>L</m> be their line of intersection.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="ex3-p6-a">
<statement>
<p>
Use the dot product to show that the two planes are perpendicular.
(1 point)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The coefficients of <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, <m>z</m> give the normal vectors
<m>\vec n_1 = \langle 1,2,2\rangle</m> and
<m>\vec n_2 = \langle 2,-2,1\rangle</m>. Two planes are perpendicular
exactly when their normal vectors are perpendicular, and
<md>
\vec n_1\cdot\vec n_2 = (1)(2) + (2)(-2) + (2)(1) = 2 - 4 + 2 = 0,
</md>
so <m>\vec n_1 \perp \vec n_2</m> and the planes are perpendicular.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="ex3-p6-b">
<statement>
<p>
Use the cross product to find a vector that is parallel to the line
<m>L</m>. (1 point)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The line <m>L</m> lies in both planes, so its direction is perpendicular to
both normal vectors. Such a vector is
<md>
<mrow>\vec n_1\times\vec n_2
\amp = \begin{vmatrix} \hat\imath \amp \hat\jmath \amp \hat k \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp -2 \amp 1 \end{vmatrix}
= (2+4)\,\hat\imath - (1-4)\,\hat\jmath + (-2-4)\,\hat k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \langle 6,3,-6\rangle = 3\,\langle 2,1,-2\rangle.</mrow>
</md>
Any non-zero multiple works; we use <m>\vec d = \langle 2,1,-2\rangle</m>
(<em>check:</em> <m>\vec d\cdot\vec n_1 = 2 + 2 - 4 = 0</m> and
<m>\vec d\cdot\vec n_2 = 4 - 2 - 2 = 0</m>).
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="ex3-p6-c">
<statement>
<p>
The point <m>A(1,0,1)</m> lies on both planes. Find parametric equations of
<m>L</m>, and an equation of the plane through the origin that is
perpendicular to <m>L</m>. (2 points)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The line <m>L</m> passes through <m>A(1,0,1)</m> and is parallel to
<m>\vec d = \langle 2,1,-2\rangle</m>:
<md>
\boxed{x(t) = 1 + 2t, \quad y(t) = t, \quad z(t) = 1 - 2t, \quad t \in \mathbb R.}
</md>
(<em>Check:</em> <m>(1+2t) + 2t + 2(1-2t) = 3</m> and
<m>2(1+2t) - 2t + (1-2t) = 3</m> for every <m>t</m>.)
</p>
<p>
A plane perpendicular to <m>L</m> has the direction vector of <m>L</m> as
its normal vector, so <m>\vec n = \langle 2,1,-2\rangle</m>. Passing through
the origin,
<md>
2(x-0) + 1(y-0) - 2(z-0) = 0, \qquad\text{that is,}\qquad
\boxed{2x + y - 2z = 0.}
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Problem 7 (bonus) ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="ex3-p7">
<title>Bonus, 2 points</title>
<statement>
<p>
A hyperbola has vertices <m>(-2,0)</m> and <m>(2,0)</m>, and its asymptotes
are the lines <m>y = \pm\frac32 x</m>. Find the equation of the hyperbola
and its foci.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The vertices lie on the <m>x</m>-axis and the center is the origin, so the
hyperbola opens left and right and has the form
<md>
\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1,
</md>
with vertices <m>(\pm a, 0)</m>, asymptotes <m>y = \pm\frac{b}{a}x</m>, and
foci <m>(\pm c, 0)</m> where <m>c^2 = a^2 + b^2</m>. The vertices give
<m>a = 2</m>, and the asymptotes give <m>\frac{b}{a} = \frac32</m>, so
<m>b = \frac32\cdot 2 = 3</m>. The hyperbola is
<md>
\boxed{\frac{x^2}{4} - \frac{y^2}{9} = 1}
\qquad \left(\text{equivalently } 9x^2 - 4y^2 = 36\right).
</md>
For the foci, <m>c^2 = a^2 + b^2 = 4 + 9 = 13</m>, so <m>c = \sqrt{13}</m>
and the foci are
<md>
\boxed{\left(-\sqrt{13}, 0\right) \text{ and } \left(\sqrt{13}, 0\right)}.
</md>
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-ex3-hyperbola">
<caption>The hyperbola <m>\frac{x^2}{4} - \frac{y^2}{9} = 1</m> with its
vertices <m>(\pm 2, 0)</m>, its foci <m>F_{1,2} = \left(\mp\sqrt{13}, 0\right)</m>
and its asymptotes <m>y = \pm\frac32 x</m>.</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-ex3-hyperbola">
<diagram dimensions="(420,360)" margins="8">
<definition>H(x,y) = x^2/4 - y^2/9</definition>
<definition>cc = sqrt(13)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-5.4, -5.6, 5.4, 5.6)">
<axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<group at="asymptotes">
<line p1="(0,0)" p2="(2,3)" stroke="gray" thickness="1" dash="5 4" infinite="yes"/>
<line p1="(0,0)" p2="(2,-3)" stroke="gray" thickness="1" dash="5 4" infinite="yes"/>
</group>
<implicit-curve at="hyperbola" function="H" k="1" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
<label at="asym-label-r" anchor="(3.4,5.1)" alignment="w" offset="(-10,0)"><m>y=\tfrac32 x</m></label>
<label at="asym-label-l" anchor="(3.4,-5.1)" alignment="w" offset="(-10,0)"><m>y=-\tfrac32 x</m></label>
<group at="vertices">
<point p="(2,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
<point p="(-2,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
</group>
<label at="v1-label" anchor="(2,0)" alignment="sw" offset="(-3,-3)"><m>2</m></label>
<label at="v2-label" anchor="(-2,0)" alignment="se" offset="(3,-3)"><m>-2</m></label>
<group at="foci">
<point p="(cc,0)" fill="red" size="3"/>
<point p="(-cc,0)" fill="red" size="3"/>
</group>
<label at="f2-label" anchor="(cc,0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-8)"><m>F_2</m></label>
<label at="f1-label" anchor="(-cc,0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-8)"><m>F_1</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The hyperbola x squared over 4 minus y squared over 9 equals 1, opening left and right, with its vertices, its foci and its two asymptotes.">
  <annotation ref="hyperbola" text="The two blue branches of the hyperbola, one opening to the right from the vertex 2 comma 0 and one opening to the left from the vertex minus 2 comma 0." sonify="yes"/>
  <annotation ref="asymptotes" text="The two dashed asymptotes through the origin, the lines y equals three halves x and y equals minus three halves x, which the branches approach."/>
  <annotation ref="vertices" text="The vertices at 2 comma 0 and minus 2 comma 0, where each branch crosses the x axis."/>
  <annotation ref="foci" text="The foci F 1 at minus the square root of 13 comma 0 and F 2 at the square root of 13 comma 0, marked in red on the x axis, beyond the vertices."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Answers at a glance ===================== -->
<assemblage xml:id="ex3-answers">
<title>Answers at a glance</title>
<p>
<ol marker="1.">
<li><p>(a) The limit does not exist: it is <m>0</m> along <m>x = 0</m> and <m>\frac12</m> along <m>y = x</m>; (b) <m>\frac12</m>.</p></li>
<li><p><m>\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial v} = 0</m>.</p></li>
<li><p><m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{(-1,-1)} = \frac{1}{\sqrt2} = \frac{\sqrt2}{2}</m>, with <m>\nabla f(-1,-1) = \langle 2,-1\rangle</m>.</p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 - y^2 - z^2 = 1</m>: a hyperboloid of two sheets along the <m>x</m>-axis, with vertices <m>(\pm 1, 0, 0)</m>.</p></li>
<li><p>False: <m>f_{xy} = 1</m> but <m>f_{yx} = 2x</m>, which contradicts the Mixed Derivative Theorem.</p></li>
<li><p>(bonus) (a) <m>\vec n_1\cdot\vec n_2 = 0</m>; (b) <m>\vec n_1\times\vec n_2 = \langle 6,3,-6\rangle</m>, parallel to <m>\langle 2,1,-2\rangle</m>; (c) <m>x = 1 + 2t</m>, <m>y = t</m>, <m>z = 1 - 2t</m>, and the plane <m>2x + y - 2z = 0</m>.</p></li>
<li><p>(bonus) <m>\frac{x^2}{4} - \frac{y^2}{9} = 1</m>, with foci <m>\left(\pm\sqrt{13}, 0\right)</m>.</p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</assemblage>

</worksheet>
